@Article(time: 5) {
  @Intro(title: "Clients") {
    Two client paths are implemented: the Google Reader API and the Fever API. Each is read as the
    set the port registers, so a path named here is a path a registration line writes, and the
    catch-all, which answers an empty document for a path no registration names, is named as the
    branch the file writes rather than as a route.

    The routes this port registers for the Google Reader path:

    - `POST /accounts/ClientLogin`
    - `GET /reader/api/0/token`
    - `GET /reader/api/0/user-info`
    - `GET /reader/api/0/tag/list`
    - `GET /reader/api/0/subscription/list`
    - `GET /reader/api/0/stream/items/ids`
    - `POST /reader/api/0/edit-tag`
    - `POST /reader/api/0/rename-tag`
    - `POST /reader/api/0/disable-tag`
    - `POST /reader/api/0/mark-all-as-read`
    - `POST /reader/api/0/subscription/edit`
    - `POST /reader/api/0/subscription/quickadd`
    - `POST /reader/api/0/stream/items/contents`

    The Fever path is registered as `GET /fever`, `POST /fever` and `HEAD /fever`, with the
    parameter read deciding which answer the handler builds, and the auth wrapper applied per route
    rather than as application middleware, so the health answer is not covered by it.

    The login reads its own field names and compares against the stored value, and a guard that an
    empty name ends the login runs before any read. The transport is chosen by method, the merged
    form follows Go's order, and a header read is read as the first value winning over the list of
    its values. Three headers are written on every response of this path, and the page size is
    clamped: a value above the clamp answers the value at the clamp.

    The clients read as data by the client matrix, and the names this page writes are the names that
    matrix records:

    - NetNewsWire
    - lire
    - Reeder Classic
    - FeedFlow
    - NewsFlash

    Only one of them has been run against this port here, through the run half that reads what one
    real client actually sent. The other four are read from their own source at a named commit, or
    from the project's documentation where the client is closed source; no set is a capture.
  }
}
